1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
In this lecture, we will discuss data hazards which exist after a processor is split up into multiple stages.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:24,000
The hazards we will cover today include program data dependencies and pipeline data hazards and the methods to resolve them including stalling and forwarding.

3
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,000
We will cover control hazards next time.

4
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
A dependence is an ordering constraint.

5
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Imagine you stop at Union South for a latte and a pastry before class.

6
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,000
You order the latte, then order the pastry, then move to the next register to check out.

7
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,000
What happens if the latte is at the register but the pastry is not there yet and you tap your whiskart?

8
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
You've only been charged for the latte and therefore receive an incomplete order.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
This is the case of the checkout instruction having a dependence upon the execution of the two order instructions before it.

10
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Now that we have multi-cycle and pipeline processors, each instruction can no longer assume it has instant and exclusive access to everything it needs when it executes.

11
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Let's look at three kinds of data dependencies in a program.

12
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,000
A true dependence is a read after write.

13
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:27,000
If instruction Y follows X and Y needs to read a result from X, then Y cannot read its registers until X produces its result.

14
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:34,000
If X reads a register before Y updates it, the program will not execute as the programmer intended it to.

15
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
The reverse of this is an anti-dependence.

16
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
This write after read is a false data dependence.

17
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:53,000
It might look like instructions X and Y are both using the same register, but as long as X reads the register before Y writes to it, we're okay.

18
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:05,000
In our five-stage pipeline, register reads happen in stage two, decode, and register writes happen in stage five, writeback, so X will always read before Y writes.

19
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:12,000
As long as our five-stage pipeline doesn't change, there's no real dependency here even though they use the same register.

20
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
The third type is an output dependence, or write after write.

21
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,000
This is also a type of a false dependence.

22
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:31,000
If instruction Y follows instruction X and they both write their results with the same register, then at the end of the sequence, the value in the register should be from instruction Y, not X.

23
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:38,000
No data flows between Y and X, but as long as the Y writeback doesn't happen before X writeback, we're okay.

24
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Let's look at an example to see these three dependencies in practice.

25
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Here we have a MIPS program with five instructions.

26
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Let's walk through and identify all the read after write registered dependencies.

27
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Here's one between instructions one and two.

28
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:09,000
The add is going to write the sum to register S3, and the read instruction is going to use S3 to calculate the address to load a word from data memory.

29
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:16,000
If instruction two were to read S3 before instruction one wrote it, the program would not execute correctly.

30
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Here's another.

31
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Instruction two is going to load a word from memory and store it in S2, and then instruction three is going to use S2 as the address to load yet another word.

32
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Once again, instruction two must write before instruction three can read.

33
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:35,000
And we have one more.

34
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Instruction four is also going to read S2, so there's another read after write dependence on instruction two.

35
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Finally, we have a RAW dependence between instructions three and four.

36
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:58,000
Instruction instructions don't write any registers, so S3 is actually a read, and that's dependent upon instruction three writing it first.

37
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:03,000
Now let's look for the WAR or write after read dependencies.

38
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:07,000
It's like RAW, but it's the reverse process.

39
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Here's a WAR dependence between instruction zero and one.

40
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Instruction zero is reading S3, and one is writing it.

41
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:23,000
It's not a true dependence, but we can't do anything in our processor that would possibly get these out of order.

42
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:35,000
Following this, we can see the other WAR dependencies going from instructions zero to three and from instruction two to three.

43
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Our last dependency category are the write after writes.

44
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Remember, these are false dependencies as long as our instructions don't execute out of order in our processor.

45
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Turns out we've only got one in this case, and that's on S3 between instructions one and three.

46
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Remember, these are a false dependence as long as we don't reorder these instructions.

47
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Pipeline hazards are potential violations of program dependence.

48
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:13,000
MIPS assembly offers a guarantee that your program will run correctly as if each statement were executed atomically or as if it would on a single cycle processor.

49
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:22,000
We've added stages and pipelining, which now puts it at risk of violating the execution guarantee if we're not careful.

50
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:25,000
There are two standard approaches to resolve hazards.

51
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:30,000
The first is statically, the compiler or programmer guarantees correctness.

52
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Imagine an assembly programmer inserting multiple no ops or idle instructions to delay a subsequent instruction and avoid a hazard.

53
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:45,000
But what if the architecture changes a bit in the next generation or in a different realization?

54
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Now a program that ran before may not run correctly now and the code is no longer portable.

55
00:05:52,000 --> 00:06:00,000
The second approach, which is what we'll focus on because we're computer architects is dynamic hazard detection,

56
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:07,000
which is to say the hardware has mechanisms to see hazards develop as instructions are decoded and to resolve them on the fly.

57
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:16,000
In general, processors have historically employed dynamic checks, though it's not unusual today to see accelerators do board the compiler space

58
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:20,000
so as to fit as many ALUs in the device as possible.

59
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Let's look at our five stage pipeline and figure out where the possible hazards are.

60
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:34,000
Remember, we have multiple instructions executing at the same time, so we've opened ourselves up to these hazards.

61
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Let's start with read after write register dependencies.

62
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:43,000
Would our pipeline ever execute incorrectly by ignoring these?

63
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Let's look at the pipeline diagram for a snippet of instructions with the known RAW dependence.

64
00:06:52,000 --> 00:07:00,000
The result of the ad is written to register one and that value is read by the subsequent sub instruction.

65
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:09,000
We know that register reads happen in stage two and writes happen in stage five and this is how our pipeline would look cycle by cycle.

66
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:18,000
It looks like subtract will read register one and cycle three, but ad won't write it until cycle five,

67
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:25,000
which means we'll violate the execution guarantees of the ISA since we can't go back in time as the arrow shows.

68
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:30,000
It's clear that read after write dependencies can lead to hazards in our pipeline.

69
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:36,000
How about write after read dependencies?

70
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:53,000
Let's take a look. In this sequence, the first instruction is reading register three and the second instruction is writing register three.

71
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:57,000
Here's the cycle by cycle timeline of these two instructions.

72
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:03,000
Add reads register three and cycle two, while sub writes register three and address six.

73
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:05,000
Do we have a problem?

74
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:10,000
The arrow goes forward in time for this dependence, so no hazard is present.

75
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:14,000
This will always be true in our pipeline since reads happen before writebacks,

76
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:19,000
so later instructions write won't ever happen before an earlier instructions read.

77
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:25,000
And finally, what about write after write dependencies?

78
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:34,000
Here, both the add and subtract write to register one and register writes happen in the writeback stage.

79
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:39,000
Since our pipeline pushes instructions through the writeback stage in order,

80
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:44,000
that means that their write after write dependence is preserved. No hazards are possible.

81
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:56,000
At this point, we've determined that only RAW register dependencies can yield hazards in our five-stage pipeline.

82
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:00,000
How about potential hazards when accessing memory?

83
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:07,000
In our pipeline, all instructions either read from or write to memory during the memory stage,

84
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:13,000
and since all instructions go through the memory stage in order, then hazards are impossible.

85
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:20,000
There's a small caveat. This could potentially be a problem if the program is able to write to a memory location that it plans to execute,

86
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:24,000
as that would mean fetch and memory are potential sources of hazards.

87
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
This is uncommon, and we will ignore it for this course.

88
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:35,000
At this point, we've discovered that only RAW hazards will pose a problem for us.

89
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
How can we maintain correctness in our pipeline?

90
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:44,000
We should first identify any hazards dynamically as early as possible.

91
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,000
Fetch is too early because we don't know what's happening yet,

92
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:54,000
but decode is where we set all of our control logic and therefore is the right spot to also detect hazards.

93
00:09:54,000 --> 00:10:02,000
Decode needs to look at the registers to be read and determine if any of the older instructions downstream are planning on writing them.

94
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:08,000
Since the control signals are propagated downstream, we can always find this information in those registers.

95
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:14,000
In step one, we'll first detect a hazard.

96
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:20,000
This sub will read register one that has a hazard from a previous write from the add.

97
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:28,000
But in our pipeline, we've decoded that sub is in our format and will read registers RS and RT,

98
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:34,000
so we just need to compare both of them with the write register in the EX-MEM pipeline registers,

99
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:37,000
which is where the add is currently active.

100
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
However, we can't stop there.

101
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:49,000
We also have to look one stage further to the EX-MEM to see if that instruction has designs in writing to one of sub's read registers.

102
00:10:50,000 --> 00:11:00,000
Since add's writes won't happen until stage five, the sub sitting in decode also has to look for a hazard three stages ahead.

103
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:09,000
So to dynamically detect a read after write hazard, the decode logic needs to look at the registers in the next three stages.

104
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:15,000
Unfortunately, that was just one case and there's some additional nuance here.

105
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:20,000
Remember that not all control signals are used all the time.

106
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:24,000
Not every instruction will write a register, like this store word.

107
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:30,000
In addition, RS and RT are not necessarily read, like with add immediate and jump.

108
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:34,000
The assigned textbook readings will discuss these in depth.

109
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:42,000
Now that we've learned how to find the information we need to detect read after write hazards,

110
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:45,000
we now need to resolve them on a live system.

111
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:52,000
The most straightforward solution is to simply stall the reader of the hazard register in the decode stage.

112
00:11:52,000 --> 00:12:00,000
In this snippet, our logic detects a hazard for register one and it needs to stop IF and ID from moving forward.

113
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:05,000
No decoding and no fetching means no new instructions are being inserted.

114
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:18,000
We need to use a NOP, aka NOOP, aka no operation, aka bubble ahead of the reader when the hazard is resolved.

115
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:29,000
When the sub detects a hazard in the execute stage, it performs a propagated stall with NOOPs until the new data is written back to the register and the hazard has been resolved.

116
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:39,000
Let's map this out in our pipeline processor diagram.

117
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:43,000
Without hazards, instructions proceed through the pipeline stage by stage.

118
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:54,000
When we detect a hazard with sub and decode, fetch and decode both freeze and a NOOP is propagated to the execute stage.

119
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:03,000
On the next clock, the same hazard check is performed. The hazard is still there, so another NOOP is inserted.

120
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:10,000
Again, the hazard is detected on the next clock, so a third straight NOOP is inserted.

121
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:20,000
At this point, the add right back to register one is completed, so the instruction can read and the sub can be propagated to the execute stage.

122
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:26,000
A note on formatting, the asterisk in the table indicates stall cycles.

123
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:35,000
Sub is sitting in decode during cycles three, four, and five, but the hazard logic is the only thing performing operations that will be kept.

124
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:39,000
It isn't until cycle six that the sub's control signals take effect.

125
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:47,000
How can we implement the stall in our processor? It requires two steps.

126
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:58,000
Step one, the right enable signals of the PC register and the fetch decode pipeline register need to be disabled, so the fetch and decode stages do not move forward.

127
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:06,000
Our processor fetches an instruction from memory every clock, so if the PC never updates, then it just refetches the same instruction.

128
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:14,000
Same with the IFID pipeline registers. If they don't trigger a strobe, they'll never set their Q output to the D input.

129
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:18,000
The clock keeps cycling, but the processor doesn't move ahead.

130
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:31,000
Step two, to insert the NOOP into the execute stage, we have to zero out the control signals to insert the NOOP, which is merely an instruction where no control happens.

131
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:42,000
In MIPS, the NOOP instruction is actually a shift left of register zero, a.k.a. the always read zero register, with a shift amount of zero.

132
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:49,000
That instruction would do nothing anyway, but since you can't write to zero, the instruction truly is idempotent.

133
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:57,000
As a brief aside, in the IFID write case, we've prevented the right strobe from propagating the signals.

134
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:05,000
In the propagating NOOP case, we must dynamically MUX in a new instruction NOOP that wasn't found in the program.

135
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:11,000
These two actions work in unison to split our stream and allow the add to eventually write back the result.

136
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:22,000
Now that we've introduced stalling into our pipeline, we risk losing the perfect IPC of one that we got with pipelining our multi-cycle processor.

137
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:32,000
As usual, computer architects are never happy with the performance and we're going to deploy all the optimizations we can to get back to our ideal IPC.

138
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:41,000
The last example saw three NOOPs inserted to ensure the writeback was committed before the next instruction was read.

139
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:47,000
We have a five-stage pipeline and we just multi-cycled over half of them to maintain correctness.

140
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:50,000
So this was a serious performance penalty indeed.

141
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:54,000
Let's introduce register file bypassing.

142
00:15:54,000 --> 00:16:00,000
In this case, we modify our register file to write on the first half of the clock and read on the second half of the clock cycle.

143
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:06,000
This means that it can look like our registers are being written to and read from simultaneously,

144
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:11,000
but the write, read, phasing keeps them correctly ordered.

145
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:17,000
In this picture, the ID and WB show which phase of the clock it's active on with the shaded portion.

146
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:23,000
ID reads the registers in the second half of the clock while WB writes them in the first half.

147
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:31,000
RF bypassing can be done with bypass paths inside the register file or by using both edges of the clock.

148
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:40,000
With this new capability, we have eliminated the third stall now that ads write and subs decode can happen simultaneously.

149
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
Now let's introduce data forwarding.

150
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
The key insights are twofold.

151
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:54,000
One, without forwarding, the data to be written exists before the writeback stage five.

152
00:16:54,000 --> 00:17:00,000
An arithmetic instruction like ADD has a known result after the execute stage three,

153
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:06,000
so there's technically no reason why you have to wait for writeback for a subsequent instruction to use it.

154
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:16,000
Two, although all instructions read their operands in the decode stage, they don't actually need them until the beginning of the execute stage.

155
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:22,000
Data forwarding is a shortcut for data to be routed back before the instruction has officially been completed,

156
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:27,000
and this can cut down on stalling due to read after write hazards.

157
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:36,000
We now need two new mitmuxes in our execute stage to add optionality to grab the most recent ALU results.

158
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:41,000
We know that the ADD result is computed by the end of cycle three.

159
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:50,000
In the beginning of cycle four, the result is sitting in the EX-MEM pipeline registers and can be forwarded back to the beginning of execute.

160
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:57,000
This is known as execute-to-execute forwarding because the result is being used by the execute stage again.

161
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:07,000
In this implementation, the decode hazard detector knows about the execute-to-execute forwarding that will happen in the future, so it did not insert no ops.

162
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:17,000
The sub-read register one, which had the old data, but it was never used as the new mux grabbed the add result and replaced it.

163
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:21,000
This eliminates all stall cycles for this particular hazard.

164
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:28,000
What happens if there's an unrelated instruction between the dependents?

165
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:33,000
In this example, in cycle five, the add is doing a writeback that the sub needs for execute.

166
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:45,000
Instead of stalling one, let's tap off the writeback bus and send a copy to those ALU input muxes we added in the last slide to create a memory-to-execute forwarding path.

167
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:54,000
In the beginning, this RAW dependents caused us to insert three stalls to maintain correctness. We've now eliminated all three.

168
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:05,000
Our last raw stall case is when the register writer is a load instruction. This is known as a load-to-use stall.

169
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:13,000
In previous forwarding optimizations, ALU results were needed for ALU inputs for a subsequent instruction.

170
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:23,000
In the load case, we don't have the data until it's read from data memory, so we can't reuse that execute-to-execute path.

171
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:31,000
We cannot avoid a single load-to-use stall, but a memory-to-execute forwarding path will prevent additional stalls.

172
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:44,000
With memory-to-execute, the sub will stall once in cycle three, but this path will enable load-word writeback and sub-execute to both happen in cycle five.

173
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:48,000
This covers the main scenarios for data forwarding.

174
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:56,000
There's one special case we're going to explore that is not in the textbook.

175
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:03,000
Earlier, we said that all operands are needed at the beginning of the execute stage. It turns out that's only mostly true.

176
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:08,000
Look at the consumers of register values and see if you can spot it.

177
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:19,000
If you said store instructions, you're right.

178
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:29,000
As shown in the figure, the data to be stored is in the RT register, but it just passes through the execute stage before being used in memory.

179
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:35,000
In your homework, you will implement memory-to-memory forwarding in order to support this special case.

180
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
Let's put this all together.

181
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:53,000
In the decode stage, we added a hazard detection unit that is able to stall the instructions in fetch and decode and is aware of the various forwarding paths that eliminate certain types of stalls.

182
00:20:54,000 --> 00:21:07,000
In the decode stage, we add a forwarding unit to control the forward muxes at the inputs of the ALU to add execute-to-execute forwarding and memory-to-execute forwarding when needed.

183
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:22,000
Once again, let's look at some exercises. When we say full forwarding and bypass, we mean execute-to-execute, memory-to-execute, and register file bypass.

184
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:32,000
In this short snippet, look at the RAW hazards and figure out where stalls, if any, are necessary. Please pause the video and work this out.

185
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:50,000
With full forwarding and bypassing, it takes nine cycles to execute this code, because this code has two load-to-use stalls, as each load is required by the instruction that follows it.

186
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:53,000
Let's look at this a different way.

187
00:21:55,000 --> 00:22:01,000
We know that stalling and instruction is the same as inserting a no-op ahead of it into the execute stage.

188
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:08,000
We also discussed how this no-op is like an instruction from the program, but the processor muxed it in out of thin air.

189
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:16,000
Once a no-op is inserted, it propagates down the pipeline until it completes doing nothing in the right backstage.

190
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:22,000
This happens again for the add in cycle 5.

191
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:28,000
Visualizing the pipeline in this stage is helpful to confirm your thinking.

192
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:38,000
Our pipeline can only hold one instruction in every stage, so adding a no-op confirms that in each column there is exactly one instruction in each of the stages.

193
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:48,000
Let's apply our pipeline cycle table to the processor diagram and walk through cycle by cycle.

194
