We'll start at cycle 3, where the second load instruction detects the raw hazard and has to issue a load-to-use stall.

195
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:09,000
Stalls insert a no-op into the execute stage at cycle 4, and the second load and add instructions are frozen in place.

196
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:24,000
Now in cycle 5, the second load can propagate to the execute stage, knowing that the result of the first load will be forwarded directly to the ALU following the memory-to-execute forwarding path.

197
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:32,000
Also, at cycle 5, the add detects its own raw hazard and inserts a load-to-use stall.

198
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:43,000
This inserts a no-op into the execute stage while the second load instruction is able to proceed.

199
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:57,000
As with the second load, the add is able to move to the execute stage in cycle 7, knowing that the memory-to-execute forwarding path will deliver the data it needs just in time.

200
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:10,000
This exercise illustrates how no-ops are virtual instructions that get executed as if they were in the original program, but did not appear until the execute stage.

201
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:15,000
One last question before we end this lecture.

202
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:19,000
Here's the memory-to-execute forwarding path highlighted.

203
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:23,000
But why not do this instead?

204
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:28,000
This would get rid of the one-cycle load-to-use stall.

205
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:30,000
Why is this a bad idea?

206
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:41,000
Remember, a clock period is based on the slowest stage of the pipeline.

207
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:50,000
So when the Q outputs change on every pipeline stage, the new values race through the logic and stabilize the D inputs on the next stage's inputs.

208
00:24:52,000 --> 00:25:02,000
The memory stage has already consumed most, if not all, of its clock period just reading the data from memory, and it needs to be registered before the next positive edge of the clock arrives.

209
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:14,000
Forwarding the result of a long combinational circuit back to the beginning of another long combinational circuit essentially tries to do two clocks worth of work in one clock.

210
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:25,000
This would never close timing in physical design, and our processor's clock rate would likely be cut roughly in half as there's a chance that it has to do two stages worth of work.

